      "dom_id": "question-103460",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-3)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-1,-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-1,-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1}{-1-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1}{-1+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$ To find the equation of this line, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3 \\\\ 4y+12 & =-3x \\\\ 3x+4y & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
